<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">American Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The American Civil War was fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the eleven southern states that seceded as the Confederacy to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The Union victory ended slavery and preserved the country. The Union <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> set off a worldwide cotton boom and blockade running, which affected places as far apart as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-civil-war.jpg" alt="American Civil War" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nathaniel Currier and James Merritt Ives · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Gettysburg,_by_Currier_and_Ives.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war grew from the desire of the Southern states to preserve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Historians in the 21st century overwhelmingly agree on the centrality of slavery in the conflict, at least for the Southern states, though they disagree on the Union's reasons for refusing to let the South secede. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>, a Republican who opposed slavery's expansion, won the presidential election of 1860, and Southern leaders feared he would put slavery on a course toward extinction.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>'s convention voted unanimously to secede on 20 December 1860. Six more Deep South states followed in January and February 1861 and seized federal forts and arsenals. The war began on 12 April 1861, when Confederate forces opened fire on Fort Sumter in Charleston harbour. Four more states seceded afterwards, so that the Confederacy, led by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Jefferson Davis</a>, comprised eleven states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> the Union captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a>, won at Fort Henry and Fort Donelson in February 1862, gaining control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> rivers. On 1 January 1863 Lincoln issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a>, which made abolition a Union war goal. The Union siege of Vicksburg split the Confederacy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a>'s incursion north failed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gettysburg">Battle of Gettysburg</a>. Announced in April 1861, a naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of Southern ports cut the South's trade.</p><p>In 1864 Lincoln made Grant commander of all Union armies. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tecumseh-sherman">William Tecumseh Sherman</a> took Atlanta on 2 September 1864 and marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a>, seizing provisions and destroying railroads. Richmond fell on 2 and 3 April 1865, and Lee surrendered to Grant at Appomattox Court House on 9 April. Lincoln was shot on 14 April and died the next day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war left an estimated 700,000 soldiers dead, along with an undetermined number of civilians, and it remains the deadliest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> history. About 190,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> served in the Union Army after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, freeing four million people. The war destroyed much of the South's wealth, including the value held in enslaved people.</p><p>Reconstruction, which lasted until 1877, rested on three amendments to the Constitution: the thirteenth outlawing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, the fourteenth guaranteeing birthright citizenship and equal protection, and the fifteenth prohibiting the denial of voting rights on account of race. The white South developed the myth of the Lost Cause, which the historian Alan T. Nolan calls a rationalisation to vindicate those in rebellion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “American Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>American Civil War</b> the Nation signed a treaty with the Confederacy on 7 October 1861.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Juárez kept a republican government alive in the north, and after the <b>American Civil War</b> the United States pressed France to withdraw.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>American Civil War</b> Abraham Lincoln’s Emancipation Proclamation of 1 January 1863 declared free the enslaved in the rebel states, some 180,000 Black men served in the Union army, and the Thirteenth Amendment, ratified in December 1865, ended slavery throughout the country.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The roots of the Harlem Renaissance reach back to the end of the <b>American Civil War</b> in 1865, when most African Americans had been enslaved in the South; in the Reconstruction era the emancipated began to strive for civic participation, political equality and economic and cultural self-determination.</blockquote><a href="/movement/harlem-renaissance">Harlem Renaissance · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…war: after the election of Abraham Lincoln in 1860, eleven southern states seceded to protect slavery and formed the Confederate States of America, and their defeat in the <b>American Civil War</b> of 1861–1865 was followed by the Fourteenth Amendment, which bound the states to respect their citizens’ rights.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Supporters point to the American record: the Constitution of the United States, in force since 1789, is the oldest written national constitution still in use, and its elections have been held on schedule even during the <b>American Civil War</b> and the Second World War.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
